  pages = {2639-2649},
  month = {FEB 29},
  abstract = {Indole (ind) is a nitrogen-containing heterocyclic volatile organic compound commonly emitted from animal husbandry and from different plants like maize with global emissions of 0.1 Tg yr - 1 . The chemical composition and optical properties of indole secondary organic aerosol (SOA) and brown carbon (BrC) are still not well understood. To address this, environmental chamber experiments were conducted to investigate the oxidation of indole at atmospherically relevant concentrations of selected oxidants (OH radicals and O 3 ) with or without NO 2 . In the presence of NO 2 , the SOA yields decreased by more than a factor of 2, but the mass absorption coefficient at 365 nm (MAC 365 ) of ind-SOA was 4.3 +/- 0.4 m 2 g - 1 , which was 5 times higher than that in experiments without NO 2 . In the presence of NO 2 , C 8 H 6 N 2 O 2 (identified as 3-nitroindole) contributed 76 \% to all organic compounds detected by a chemical ionization mass spectrometer, contributing similar to 50 \% of the light absorption at 365 nm (Abs 365 ). In the absence of NO 2 , the dominating chromophore was C 8 H 7 O 3 N, contributing to 20 \%-30 \% of Abs 365 . Indole contributes substantially to the formation of secondary BrC and its potential impact on the atmospheric radiative transfer is further enhanced in the presence of NO 2 , as it significantly increases the specific light absorption of ind-SOA by facilitating the formation of 3-nitroindole. This work provides new insights into an important process of brown carbon formation by interaction of two pollutants, NO 2 and indole, mainly emitted by anthropogenic activities.},
